# KNOWN GAP — C BACKEND ONLY; LLVM is CORRECT (lane divergence, Core #9).
#
# `Shared[String]("a literal")` — the exact form `docs/language-design.md:1846`
# teaches — passes `gg check`, then the C backend emits
#
#     Shared__GorgetString__new((const char*)__v0.data)
#
# against the wrapper it emitted one line earlier,
#
#     static inline Shared__GorgetString Shared__GorgetString__new(Str val)
#
# and `cc` refuses: `error: incompatible type for argument 1 of
# 'Shared__GorgetString__new'`. The `Str`-typed constructor parameter is
# marshalled as if it were tagged `CStr`. `--backend=llvm` builds and prints
# `hi`, so the C emit boundary is the lane that is wrong.
#
# Feeding the SAME constructor a `String` VARIABLE compiles and runs on both
# lanes, which is why `shared_vector_payload_types.gg` builds its payload from
# a concatenation — that is a dodge around THIS gap, and it is recorded here
# rather than buried in that fixture.
#
# NOT the `Vector[Shared[T]]` handle-marshalling class (todo/t0840): no
# container, no element slot, no `AbiKind::VoidElem` position — this is
# argument marshalling into a synthesized inline wrapper.
#
# INTENDED (asserted by the wired test): prints `hi`.

void main():
    Shared[String] s = Shared[String]("hi")
    print(f"{s.get()}")
